    "title": "pandas.PeriodIndex.day_of_year — pandas 3.1.0.dev0+1661.gf74cda2e6e documentation",
    "status": 200,
    "markdown": "# pandas.PeriodIndex.day_of_year#\n\n- property PeriodIndex.day_of_year[source]#\n- The ordinal day of the year. Returns the day-of-year component for each period, ranging from 1 (January 1st) to 365 or 366 for leap years. See also \n  - `PeriodIndex.day`\n  - The days of the period.\n  - `PeriodIndex.day_of_week`\n  - The day of the week with Monday=0, Sunday=6.\n  - `PeriodIndex.day_of_year`\n  - The ordinal day of the year.\n  - `PeriodIndex.dayofweek`\n  - The day of the week with Monday=0, Sunday=6.\n  - `PeriodIndex.dayofyear`\n  - The ordinal day of the year.\n  - `PeriodIndex.weekday`\n  - The day of the week with Monday=0, Sunday=6.\n  - `PeriodIndex.weekofyear`\n  - The week ordinal of the year.\n  - `PeriodIndex.year`\n  - The year of the period.\n Examples >>> idx = pd.PeriodIndex([\"2023-01-10\", \"2023-02-01\", \"2023-03-01\"], freq=\"D\") >>> idx.day_of_year Index([10, 32, 60], dtype='int64') >>> idx = pd.PeriodIndex([\"2023\", \"2024\", \"2025\"], freq=\"Y\") >>> idx PeriodIndex(['2023', '2024', '2025'], dtype='period[Y-DEC]') >>> idx.day_of_year Index([365, 366, 365], dtype='int64')",
    "markdown_length": 1080,
